1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,175
Ask a motion diffusion model for a person walking forward slowly and carefully, and watch closely.

2
00:00:07,625 --> 00:00:10,000
Most samples are fine.

3
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:20,700
But in some, in the middle of a step, both feet leave the floor and the whole body glides a few centimeters up, for half a second, before it sets down again.

4
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:26,575
Nothing pulls it back down, because nothing in the model enforces the ground.

5
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:37,125
Kinematic motion generators output joint positions directly, so their physics is learned from motion capture and never enforced.

6
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:45,625
Video diffusion models were recently reported to know more physics in their activations than they show in their outputs.

7
00:00:46,075 --> 00:00:52,425
If motion models did too, a probe on the layers could catch a sample like that one early.

8
00:00:52,875 --> 00:00:55,600
We set out to show exactly that.

9
00:00:56,050 --> 00:00:58,325
It is not what we found.

10
00:00:59,675 --> 00:01:10,775
A diffusion model builds a motion out of noise in many small steps, and at every step it computes a clean-motion estimate: its running guess of the final motion.

11
00:01:11,225 --> 00:01:14,625
Here is one jump at four points of denoising.

12
00:01:15,075 --> 00:01:18,925
At zero percent the estimate is an average crouch.

13
00:01:19,375 --> 00:01:27,650
By twenty percent the jump is in place, and it falls at nine and a half meters per second squared, close to gravity.

14
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:34,150
Slowed down, the estimate at twenty percent moves almost on top of the final motion.

15
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:51,625
Because the output is joint positions, physics can be computed exactly, for the final motion and for every estimate: balance from the extrapolated center of mass, and for flight, a parabola fitted to the center of mass in the air.

16
00:01:52,075 --> 00:01:58,650
A real jump falls at nine point eight one; a body that falls slower than two is floating.

17
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:08,475
Our first definition of "airborne" looked only at the feet and counted crawling and hovering as flight; the corrected labels separate them.

18
00:02:09,825 --> 00:02:11,750
Here is the catch.

19
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:21,475
The estimate is computed from the model's layers, so it cannot carry information about the outcome that the layers and the noisy input lack.

20
00:02:21,925 --> 00:02:29,850
A probe that beats a quick readout of the estimate only shows that the layers store the information in an easier form.

21
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:39,025
So for every target we train two readers, one on the estimate and one on the estimate and a layer, both tuned the same way.

22
00:02:39,475 --> 00:02:42,975
The gap between them is what the layer adds.

23
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:50,850
Against the usual baselines, the layers look ahead of the estimate on almost every target.

24
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:56,275
Read the estimate with the same care, and the advantage disappears.

25
00:02:56,725 --> 00:03:05,600
For balance and flight, on all three models, adding a layer gains at most one hundredth, one point on a hundred-point scale.

26
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:14,825
A positive control, in which we hide a small known signal in the layer, shows that the protocol would have found a gain that small.

27
00:03:16,175 --> 00:03:22,550
Our pilot study, with sixty-four prompts, pointed the other way, and the reason was data.

28
00:03:22,1000 --> 00:03:30,325
With too few training examples, a high-dimensional probe and a one-number baseline can trade places.

29
00:03:30,775 --> 00:03:36,850
Only a lead that persists as the training set grows is evidence about the model.

30
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:42,000
There is one exception: how fast a jump falls.

31
00:03:42,450 --> 00:03:56,350
A fifth of the way through denoising, MDM's layers add about eight hundredths in R squared that the estimate does not yet show, and on the fifty-step model the gain survives every control we applied.

32
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:01,825
But it fades: by sixty percent of the way it is under two hundredths.

33
00:04:02,275 --> 00:04:08,125
A real case of a model knowing more than it shows, but a small and short-lived one.

34
00:04:22,275 --> 00:04:24,250
What does go wrong?

35
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:30,800
Mostly not the jumps: when MDM's bodies fall, they fall at close to the right rate.

36
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:40,775
The common failure is floating, and on test captions the original thousand-step checkpoint floats about twice as often as the fifty-step one.

37
00:04:41,225 --> 00:04:46,550
The estimate at twenty percent already floats where the final motion does.

38
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:52,750
That makes the estimate an early warning, with no training at all.

39
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:59,525
If it floats at twenty percent, the final motion floats in about eighty percent of cases.

40
00:04:59,975 --> 00:05:02,200
But the payoff is small.

41
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:13,150
Floating affects only nine to eighteen percent of samples and concentrates in a few prompts, so aborting early saves under a tenth of the compute.

42
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:16,850
Even a perfect detector would save little.

43
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:27,500
In video, a linear probe at chance on Wan two point one's clean latent was read as evidence that plausibility is absent there.

44
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:38,325
One fixed transformation, the size of the change between frames, lifts a linear readout to seventy-eight percent pairwise accuracy on IntPhys.

45
00:05:38,775 --> 00:05:42,525
A probe at chance is weak evidence of absence.

46
00:05:43,875 --> 00:05:50,025
Before concluding that a generator knows more than it shows, we suggest five checks.

47
00:05:50,475 --> 00:05:56,250
Read its own estimate as carefully as its layers, and run a positive control.

48
00:05:56,700 --> 00:06:01,200
Score within prompts, and vary the amount of training data.

49
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:06,150
Try simple transforms before declaring something absent.

50
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:09,975
Look at what a target's variance is made of.

51
00:06:10,425 --> 00:06:15,300
And for early pruning, compare with scoring the decoded preview.

52
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:23,550
Every body, path and number in this video is computed from the recorded outputs of the models.

